---
import PageLayout from "@/layouts/PageLayout.astro";
import Prose from "@/components/astro/Prose.astro";
import { DISCLAIMER, PRIVACY_PROMISE } from "@/lib/site";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<PageLayout
  title="How Chat Analysis Works — ChatWrapped’s Metric Definitions"
  description="How ChatWrapped turns chat exports into statistics: the normalized message model, response-time burst rules, conversation starts, emoji counting and timezone handling — all documented."
  path="/how-chat-analysis-works/"
>
  <Prose />
  <main id="main" class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-12">
    <h1 class="font-display text-4xl font-bold text-white">How Chat Analysis Works</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-zinc-300">
      Most chat tools show numbers without explaining where they come from. ChatWrapped does the
      opposite: every metric has a written definition, explicit exclusions and tested edge cases.
      This page is the short version; the complete reference lives in the repository at
      <code>docs/analytics/METRIC_DEFINITIONS.md</code>.
    </p>

    <div class="prose-cw">
      <h2>Step 1 — Parsing into a normalized model</h2>
      <p>
        Both supported formats (WhatsApp TXT, Telegram JSON) are parsed into one internal message
        model: participant, timestamp, text, type (text / media / system) and attachment flag. This
        is why the same statistics and stories work identically for both platforms. Parsing handles
        multiline messages, colons in text, Unicode directionality marks, attachment placeholders,
        deletion markers and system events.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 2 — The eligible pool</h2>
      <p>
        Statistics are computed over the <strong>eligible pool</strong>: non-system messages from
        included participants, inside the selected date range. You always see both the raw parsed
        count and the eligible count, so exclusions are never hidden. Group noise (joins, pins,
        calls, “this message was deleted”) never pollutes the numbers.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 3 — Timestamps without timezone lies</h2>
      <p>
        Exports store <strong>local wall-clock time with no timezone metadata</strong>. ChatWrapped
        interprets the wall clock as UTC — a constant offset — which keeps every <em>duration</em> exact
        (response times, gaps, streaks) and keeps every <em>bucket</em> faithful to what the sender saw
        on their clock. No timezone is ever invented. WhatsApp’s ambiguous DD/MM vs MM/DD formats are
        resolved from unambiguous evidence and flagged for you to confirm when the data can’t decide.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 4 — Response times, done honestly</h2>
      <p>
        Naive tools treat every message pair as a reply, which collapses when people double-text.
        ChatWrapped’s rules:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Burst:</strong> consecutive messages by the same participant within 120 seconds form
          one burst.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Response event:</strong> the first message from a different participant after a burst,
          measured from the burst’s first message.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Validity:</strong> only responses within 6 hours enter averages and medians; longer
          silences are tracked separately as the longest gap.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>Boundaries are unit-tested (exactly 6 hours counts; 6 hours + 1 ms does not).</p>

      <h2>Step 5 — Conversation starts</h2>
      <p>
        A conversation “starts” after a lull of at least <strong>4 hours</strong> since the previous eligible
        message — plus the very first message of the selection. The starter is credited. This is why “Conversation
        Starter” awards need at least three starts.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 6 — Emoji and words</h2>
      <p>
        Emoji are counted as <strong>grapheme clusters</strong> via <code>Intl.Segmenter</code>:
        skin tones, ZWJ families, flags and keycaps are one emoji each — never misleading fragments.
        Words are segmented per locale, lowercased and filtered (stopwords, numbers, URLs, emails)
        for signature-word rankings.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 7 — Stories, records and awards</h2>
      <p>
        Slides are generated adaptively from the computed analytics — a slide for a missing metric
        is skipped, never fabricated. Records require sufficient data; awards have explicit
        thresholds (published in the metric definitions) and ties are reported as ties. Small
        samples produce a visible “small sample” note.
      </p>

      <h2>What the analysis refuses to do</h2>
      <blockquote>
        {PRIVACY_PROMISE} And no statistic implies anything about love, loyalty, personality or relationship
        quality — ChatWrapped counts events; people make meanings.
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        Curious how this plays out on real data? Open the <a href={`${base}/app/`}>analyzer</a> — the
        sample data is synthetic and clearly labeled, so you can explore every screen without exporting
        anything.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-10 text-xs leading-relaxed text-zinc-600">{DISCLAIMER}</p>
  </main>
</PageLayout>
